---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CommitSong from '../../components/CommitSong.astro';
import { recentCommits } from '../../lib/git.ts';
import { commitParams, progression } from '../../lib/commitParams.ts';

const initial = recentCommits(1)[0]?.hash ?? 'hello world';
---

<Base title="Commit Song Machine" description="Turn any commit hash or text into a little song and a picture.">
  <div class="page-intro">
    <h1>Commit Song Machine</h1>
    <p>Paste a commit hash, or type anything, to get a chord progression and a picture of it.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="panel machine">
    <form class="machine-input" data-machine-form>
      <label class="visually-hidden" for="hash">Commit hash or any text</label>
      <input id="hash" name="hash" value={initial} autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" />
      <button type="button" class="pill-btn ghost" data-random>Random</button>
    </form>
    <CommitSong hash={initial} size="full" />
    <p class="progression" data-progression>{progression(commitParams(initial))}</p>
  </section>

  <details class="panel machine explainer">
    <summary>
      <span class="explainer-title">How it works</span>
      <span class="explainer-hint">Expand to learn how this works</span>
    </summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every post on this site gets a song from the git commit that created it. This machine is the same generator, and
        you can feed it anything. The same text always makes the same song, note for note.
      </p>

      <h2>From text to seed</h2>
      <p>
        Whatever you type is hashed into four 32-bit numbers, which seed a small random number generator. Every choice
        below is a roll of that generator, made in a fixed order. Change one character and every roll changes, so you get
        a completely different song.
      </p>

      <h2>The harmony</h2>
      <p>
        The song is four bars long: eight chords, half a bar each, which loop. First it picks a key (any of the twelve)
        and a progression from a small library of classic ones. The library leans dark: most of the progressions are in
        minor modes.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>i–VI–III–VII, the wistful minor loop</li>
        <li>the Andalusian cadence, i–VII–VI–V, walking down to a major V</li>
        <li>i–VI–iv–V</li>
        <li>the minor circle of fifths, eight chords long</li>
        <li>i–iv–VII–III and iv–VI–VII–i</li>
        <li>dorian i–III–VII–IV, and a two-chord dorian vamp</li>
        <li>two phrygian progressions built on the dark ♭II</li>
        <li>and, rarely, a bittersweet major I–vi–IV–iv or a floating lydian I–II</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each chord is built by stacking thirds within the mode, as a 7th or a 9th. Some progressions borrow a chord from
        outside the mode, like a major V in a minor key, or a minor iv in a major one. A few chords get a suspended
        colour, but never the home chord or a borrowed one. Each chord is spelled once, so a four-chord progression
        repeats exactly the second time round.
      </p>
      <p>
        Chords are kept to four notes and voiced open, spread across about an octave and a half. The fifth is the first
        note to go, because it adds the least colour. Each voicing is the one closest to the previous chord, so the
        harmony moves smoothly instead of jumping around.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most songs are pure dream. About four in ten carry some unease. Their tape wobble is deeper, and once the loop
        has been heard, one chord in the second half may be swapped for something strange: a chromatic root with an
        out-of-key colour, slightly out of tune.
      </p>

      <h2>The performance</h2>
      <p>
        Each song plays its chords one of four ways: held as a pad, strummed note by note, arpeggiated throughout, or
        pulsed in a seeded rhythm. Songs that aren't arpeggiated get a soft arpeggio over the chords for two bars, one in
        each half. The tempo is somewhere between 76 and 112 bpm. The sound is one of four synth voices: velvet (detuned
        saws), glass (bell-like), hollow (woody and a little eerie) or choir (a slow organ haze). The bass holds each
        chord's root underneath.
      </p>

      <h2>The drums</h2>
      <p>
        The beat is one of four grooves: backbeat, half-time, four on the floor or shuffle. It repeats every bar, and
        the last bar ends with a small fill: a snare roll, a kick pickup, or a hi-hat that drops out. Sometimes bar 3
        takes a breath. A lone kick lands on the downbeat, and the chord comes in late.
      </p>

      <h2>The sound</h2>
      <p>
        Everything passes through a slow tape wobble that bends the pitch, plus reverb and a delay timed to the tempo.
        The chords also duck on every kick and swell back between hits, and a slow phaser sweeps through them. The speed
        and depth of each effect are seeded too, so some songs barely move and others churn.
      </p>

      <h2>Reading the picture</h2>
      <p>
        The picture is the score. Time runs left to right, and height is pitch. Tails show how long a note rings, and
        each chord gets its own colour. Shapes show each chord's feeling: ● major, ■ minor, ▲ suspended, and jagged for
        the strange ones. Below that is the bass, then the drums: dots are hi-hats, diamonds snare, squares kick. The
        same numbers drive the picture and the sound, so what you see is what you hear.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
</Base>

<script>
  import { commitParams, describe, progression } from '../../lib/commitParams.ts';
  import { renderCommitArt } from '../../lib/commitArt.ts';
  import { stopCommitSong } from '../../lib/commitPlayer.ts';

  const form = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-machine-form]')!;
  const input = form.querySelector('input')!;
  const song = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-commit-song]')!;

  function update() {
    const hash = input.value.trim() || ' ';
    if (hash === song.dataset.hash) return;
    stopCommitSong();
    const params = commitParams(hash);
    song.dataset.hash = hash;
    song.querySelector('.art-frame')!.innerHTML = renderCommitArt(params);
    const meta = song.querySelector('[data-song-meta]')!;
    meta.innerHTML = '<code></code> · ';
    meta.querySelector('code')!.textContent = hash.slice(0, 7);
    meta.append(describe(params));
    document.querySelector('[data-progression]')!.textContent = progression(params);
  }

  input.addEventListener('input', update);
  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => e.preventDefault());
  form.querySelector('[data-random]')!.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const bytes = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(20));
    input.value = Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
    update();
  });
</script>
